9': 194, '2020': 179, '2021': 216, '2022': 213, '2023': 215}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\n# Season ranges per file\ndef seasons(path, col):\n    c = Counter()\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n            c[row[col]] += 1\n    return c\n\nprint(\"Brasileirao:\", dict(sorted(seasons('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"Copa do Brasil:\", dict(sorted(seasons('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"Libertadores:\", dict(sorted(seasons('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv','season').items())))\nprint(\"novo:\", dict(sorted(seasons('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','Ano').items())))\nprint(\"BR-Football:\", dict(sorted(seasons('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','tournament').items())))\nd = defaultdict(Counter)\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        d[row['tournament']][row['date'][:4]] += 1\nfor t, c in d.items():\n    print(\"BRFB\", t, dict(sorted(c.items())))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788273134893,"end":1788273134896}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good — MCP 2.x `MCPServer` has a `tool` decorator and `call_tool` method. I can use this API directly. Now let me design the implementation:\n\n**Architecture:**\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py\n  server.py          # MCP server with tool definitions\n  data_loader.py     # Load and parse all 6 CSV files\n  models.py          # Data classes: Match, Player, TeamStats\n  normalize.py       # Team name normalization, date parsing\n  queries.py         # Query functions (matches, teams, players, standings, stats)\ntests/\n  conftest.py        # Fixtures - loaded data\n  test_bdd_match_queries.py\n  test_bdd_team_queries.py\n  test_bdd_player_queries.py\n  test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n  test_bdd_statistics.py\n  test_mcp_server.py\n  test_normalize.py\nrun_server.py / server entry point\nrequirements.txt / pyproject.toml\n```\n\nKey design decisions:\n1. **Pure Python + stdlib csv** — no pandas needed (fast load, fewer deps). Actually pandas isn't installed and I shouldn't rely on network for install... wait, I already installed mcp from network successfully. Let me keep dependencies minimal: only `mcp` for the server. Use stdlib `csv` for data loading — that's simple and fast enough (42k rows).\n\n2. **Team name normalization**: strip state suffixes (\"-SP\"), handle accents (unicode NFKD or unicodedata), lowercase, remove punctuation. Build alias tables for known variants: \"Athletico-PR\" vs \"Atlético-PR\" vs \"Paraná\"... Let me look at actual variants across files first. Key normalizations:\n   - `America-MG` vs `América - MG` vs `América-MG`\n   - `Atletico-MG` vs `Atlético-MG` (accent)\n   - `São Paulo` vs `Sao Paulo`\n   - `Corinthians-SP` vs `Corinthians`\n   - `Athletico-PR` vs `Atlético-PR` (in 2019+, Atlético-PR renamed to Athletico-PR)\n   - Full names: \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" (fifa Club names might be different)\n\n3. **Date parsing**: handle \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"29/03/2003\", \"2023-09-24\".\n\n4. **MCP tools** (to cover the required capabilities):\n   - `search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit)` — match queries\n   - `head_to_head(team_a, team_b)` — h2h record\n   - `team_stats(team, competition, season, venue)` — team queries (home/away/either)\n   - `search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, limit)` — player queries\n   - `top_players(...)` — highest-rated players by club/nationality\n   - `standings(competition, season)` — competition standings calculated from matches\n   - `competition_info()` — list competitions & seasons available\n   - `biggest_wins(competition, season, limit)` — statistical analysis\n   - `average_goals(competition, season)` — goals per match\n   - `home_away_splits`/`best_team_record(venue, competition, season)` — home vs away performance\n   - `derbies(season)` — derby matches (needs a derby/rivals table)\n   - `last_match_between(team_a, team_b)` — \"when did X last play Y\"\n   - `team_competitions(team)` — what competitions has a team played in\n\nThat covers all 5 categories plus sample questions. Should be well over 20 answerable questions.\n\n5. **BDD tests**: pytest with GWT (Given-When-Then) structured tests. I'll write docstrings in Gherkin style with given/when/then structure in test names and comments. E.g.:\n\n```python\nclass TestFindMatchesBetweenTwoTeams:\n    \"\"\"Feature: Match Queries\n\n    Scenario: Find matches between two teams\n      Given the match data is loaded\n      When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\n      Then I should receive a list of matches\n      And each match should have date, scores, and competition\n    \"\"\"\n    def test_given_match_data_loaded_when_search_flamengo_fluminense_then_returns_matches(self, loaded_data):\n        ...\n```\n\n6. **Derby table**: Classic Brazilian derbies: Fla-Flu (Flamengo vs Fluminense), Clássico dos Milhões (Flamengo vs Vasco), Gre-nal (Grêmio vs Internacional), Clássico Majestoso (Corinthians vs São Paulo), Choque-Rei (São Paulo vs Santos), Clássico Alvinegro... Let me include a good set of famous derbies with names.\n\n7. **Standings calculation**: For Brasileirão (both files) — double round-robin, 3 points per win. Need to be careful with the historical file (2003-2019) — in 2003-2005 some seasons had playoffs... Actually, simpler: just compute points from all matches in the season. The spec says \"Standings by season (calculated from match results)\" — straightforward points table is what they want (their example shows 2019 Flamengo 90 pts 28W 6D 4L = 38 matches, which is exactly a double round robin with 20 teams).\n\nWait, but the historical data might duplicate matches with the other file. Brasileirao_Matches.csv is 2012+ (4,180 matches). novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv is 2003-2019 (6,886 matches). So there's overlap 2012-2019. The datasets are separate \"sources\" — I should keep them as separate competitions/sources. Hmm. For standings, if I compute 2019 standings from novo file, will I get Flamengo 90 pts? Let me check: 2019 Brasileirão Flamengo: 28W 6D 4L, 90 pts. Let me verify with data during testing. If both files contain 2019, I need to decide which to use for standings. I'll treat each file as a distinct \"source\" but expose competitions as logical entities:\n- \"Brasileirão\" (Serie A) from Brasileirao_Matches.csv (2012-2023?)\n- \"Brasileirão (2003-2019)\" from novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv\n- \"Copa do Brasil\" from Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv (and also BR-Football-Dataset has Copa do Brasil, Serie A/B/C)\n- \"Libertadores\" from Libertadores_Matches.csv\n- \"BR Football\" extended stats dataset with Serie A, Serie B, Serie C, Copa do Brasil\n\nLet me check season ranges in each file. Let me also check for duplicates within Brasileirão files. Actually, to keep it clean: I'll model a unified `Match` record with `source` and `competition` fields, and dedupe when computing standings? The overlap 2012-2019 between the two Brasileirão files would double-count. Hmm.\n\nOption: For standings and stats, allow specifying which source/competition, defaulting to a canonical one. Or dedupe matches across files by (date, home, away, score). Let me check the overlap.\n\nLet me look at the seasons:\n- Brasileirao_Matches.csv: 2012-? (4180 matches; 2023 season has 380 matches per season for 20 teams; from 2012 to 2023 = 12 seasons... 2012-2015 had 20 teams (380/season), 2016-2020... hmm whatever, let me check).\n- novo: 2003-2019.\n\nI'll check quickly. Also check whether the two files agree on overlapping matches (for dedupe).\n\nPlan for dedup: I'll add a `dedupe` step when querying across \"all competitions\" or computing stats when both sources are involved. Actually simpler and more honest: keep sources separate; each query filters by competition which maps to specific source(s). For \"Brasileirão\" competition default, use the newer Brasileirao_Matches.csv file (2012+). For historical queries (2003-2019), the novo file is there. But then \"who won the 2019 Brasileirão\" would ideally use novo (since Brasileirao_Matches may also have 2019). Let me check if Brasileirao_Matches.csv includes 2019 — if so, either works.\n\nActually, let me make the competition mapping:\n- `brasileirao` → Brasileirao_Matches.csv (2012-2023)\n- `brasileirao_historic` or... hmm.\n\nWait — actually let me check if the two files' Brasileirão data can just be merged with dedup by (season, round, home, away). The novo file has round (Rodada), the other has round too. If both have \"2019 Flamengo vs ...\" with same round, dedup works. Let me check overlap.\n\nLet me do the data exploration now, then decide. Let me also check the BR-Football-Dataset date range and tournament contents.\n\nThen implement:\n\n**Files:**\n1. `brazilian_soccer/__init__.py`\n2. `brazilian_soccer/models.py` — dataclasses\n3. `brazilian_soccer/normalize.py` — name normalization, date parsing, derby table\n4. `brazilian_soccer/loader.py` — CSV loaders for all 6 files → unified Match/Player lists\n5. `brazilian_soccer/queries.py` — query engine (search matches, h2h, team stats, standings, players, biggest wins, averages, derbies, last match, etc.)\n6. `brazilian_soccer/server.py` — MCPServer with @tool definitions, formatters for response text\n7. `server.py` (entry) or `main.py` — `python main.py` runs the MCP server over stdio\n8. `tests/` — BDD GWT tests\n9. `requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml` maybe\n10. Update README.md\n\nAlso the prompts.txt mentions \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — that was a sample prompt for the benchmark, not necessarily my instructions. My instruction is just \"Read TASK.md ... implement everything it asks for\". The TASK.md doesn't require context block comments. I'll write clean code with good docstrings. Since the system prompt says \"DO NOT ADD ANY COMMENTS unless asked\" — I'll use docstrings where they serve as documentation (needed for MCP tool descriptions anyway) but avoid inline comments.\n\nLet me now explore the data more.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d628cc8001hk81dvjeuH3Itv","sessionID":"ses_fa29f8b64ffekf1eHikDs909KG","messageID":"msg_05d61d20b001I0EnBwrypGFcFf"}}
