remio\" alone occurs (novo + BRFB \"Gremio\") — and \"Gremio RS\" in BRFB. Same club, alias \"gremio rs\" → \"gremio\".\n\nThe alias dict needs to be built from the actual distinct names. Since there are 725 raw names, most will be fine with algorithmic normalization + a curated alias list for the known-same variants (which I enumerated above by eyeballing). Perfect dedup of every tiny Serie D club isn't required for the benchmark, but big clubs must be right.\n\n**FIFA club names**: also need normalization for cross-file queries (players + matches). FIFA names: \"Grêmio\", \"Santos\", \"Internacional\", \"Botafogo\", \"Fluminense\", \"Bahia\", \"Cruzeiro\". Some FIFA clubs not in match data (e.g., \"Palmeiras\" — wait, \"Palmeiras\" wasn't in the Brazilian-ish list I filtered? Let me re-check — my filter had 'Palmeiras' in the list of keys and it printed only: Bahia, Botafogo, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Grêmio, Internacional, Santos, Santos Laguna. So FIFA 2019 data has NO Palmeiras, Flamengo, Corinthians, São Paulo, Vasco, Athletico-PR, Fortaleza players?! Interesting — the FIFA dataset is FIFA 19/20 era. Flamengo players might be listed... hmm, \"Gabriel Barbosa\" — spec says \"Who is Gabriel Barbosa? Search FIFA player data by name\". Let me check what club Gabriel Barbosa has in fifa_data. Also check \"Palmeiras\" exactly. If absent, then \"highest-rated players at Flamengo\" example might return empty — need to verify. Let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d6480ed001bS9PQKl0gzAWDt","sessionID":"ses_fa29f8b64ffekf1eHikDs909KG","messageID":"msg_05d6387d90010ZvfsPTv8Id4ZI"}}
