{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788273346223,"sessionID":"ses_fa29f8b64ffekf1eHikDs909KG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_711074446eea46498b69c503","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nsrc = inspect.getsource(MCPServer.tool)\nprint(src[:3500])\nEOF"},"output":"    def tool(\n        self,\n        name: str | None = None,\n        title: str | None = None,\n        description: str | None = None,\n        annotations: ToolAnnotations | None = None,\n        icons: list[Icon] | None = None,\n        meta: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\n        structured_output: bool | None = None,\n    ) -> Callable[[_CallableT], _CallableT]:\n        \"\"\"Decorator to register a tool.\n\n        Tools can optionally request a Context object by adding a parameter with the\n        Context type annotation. The context provides access to MCP capabilities like\n        logging, progress reporting, and resource access.\n\n        Args:\n            name: Optional name for the tool (defaults to function name)\n            title: Optional human-readable title for the tool\n            description: Optional description of what the tool does\n            annotations: Optional ToolAnnotations providing additional tool information\n            icons: Optional list of icons for the tool\n            meta: Optional metadata dictionary for the tool\n            structured_output: Controls whether the tool's output is structured or unstructured\n                - If None, auto-detects based on the function's return type annotation\n                - If True, creates a structured tool (return type annotation permitting)\n                - If False, unconditionally creates an unstructured tool\n\n        Example:\n            ```python\n            @server.tool()\n            def my_tool(x: int) -> str:\n                return str(x)\n            ```\n\n            ```python\n            @server.tool()\n            async def tool_with_context(x: int, ctx: Context) -> str:\n                await ctx.info(f\"Processing {x}\")\n                return str(x)\n            ```\n\n            ```python\n            @server.tool()\n            async def async_tool(x: int, context: Context) -> str:\n                await context.report_progress(50, 100)\n                return str(x)\n            ```\n        \"\"\"\n        # Check if user passed function directly instead of calling decorator\n        if callable(name):\n            raise TypeError(\n                \"The @tool decorator was used incorrectly. Did you forget to call it? Use @tool() instead of @tool\"\n            )\n\n        def decorator(fn: _CallableT) -> _CallableT:\n            self.add_tool(\n                fn,\n                name=name,\n                title=title,\n                description=description,\n                annotations=annotations,\n                icons=icons,\n                meta=meta,\n                structured_output=structured_output,\n            )\n            return fn\n\n        return decorator\n\n","metadata":{"output":"    def tool(\n        self,\n        name: str | None = None,\n        title: str | None = None,\n        description: str | None = None,\n        annotations: ToolAnnotations | None = None,\n        icons: list[Icon] | None = None,\n        meta: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\n        structured_output: bool | None = None,\n    ) -> Callable[[_CallableT], _CallableT]:\n        \"\"\"Decorator to register a tool.\n\n        Tools can optionally request a Context object by adding a parameter with the\n        Context type annotation. The context provides access to MCP capabilities like\n        logging, progress reporting, and resource access.\n\n        Args:\n            name: Optional name for the tool (defaults to function name)\n            title: Optional human-readable title for the tool\n            description: Optional description of what the tool does\n            annotations: Optional ToolAnnotations providing additional tool information\n            icons: Optional list of icons for the tool\n            meta: Optional metadata dictionary for the tool\n            structured_output: Controls whether the tool's output is structured or unstructured\n                - If None, auto-detects based on the function's return type annotation\n                - If True, creates a structured tool (return type annotation permitting)\n                - If False, unconditionally creates an unstructured tool\n\n        Example:\n            ```python\n            @server.tool()\n            def my_tool(x: int) -> str:\n                return str(x)\n            ```\n\n            ```python\n            @server.tool()\n            async def tool_with_context(x: int, ctx: Context) -> str:\n                await ctx.info(f\"Processing {x}\")\n                return str(x)\n            ```\n\n            ```python\n            @server.tool()\n            async def async_tool(x: int, context: Context) -> str:\n                await context.report_progress(50, 100)\n                return str(x)\n            ```\n        \"\"\"\n        # Check if user passed function directly instead of calling decorator\n        if callable(name):\n            raise TypeError(\n                \"The @tool decorator was used incorrectly. Did you forget to call it? Use @tool() instead of @tool\"\n            )\n\n        def decorator(fn: _CallableT) -> _CallableT:\n            self.add_tool(\n                fn,\n                name=name,\n                title=title,\n                description=description,\n                annotations=annotations,\n                icons=icons,\n                meta=meta,\n                structured_output=structured_output,\n            )\n            return fn\n\n        return decorator\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nsrc = inspect.getsource(MCPServer.tool)\nprint(src[:3500])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788273346189,"end":1788273346222}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"FIFA dataset has these Brazilian clubs (20 players each): Atlético Mineiro, Atlético Paranaense, Bahia, Botafogo, Ceará Sporting Club, Chapecoense, Cruzeiro, Fluminense, Grêmio, Internacional, Paraná, Santos, Sport Club do Recife, Vitória.\n\nSo FIFA club aliases needed:\n- \"Atlético Mineiro\" → atlético-mg\n- \"Atlético Paranaense\" → atlético-pr (Athletico-PR)\n- \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → ceará\n- \"Sport Club do Recife\" → sport (recife)\n- \"Vitória\" → vitória (BA) — FIFA \"Vitória\" is EC Vitória (BA). And \"Vitória Guimarães\"/\"Vitória de Setúbal\" are Portuguese clubs — my matcher must handle \"Vitória\" exactly.\n- \"Paraná\" → paraná\n- \"Grêmio\" → grêmio, etc.\n\nNow for the fifa \"Value\"/\"Wage\" fields (€110.5M, €565K) — could parse but not required. Age, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, Jersey Number, Height, Weight are the key ones.\n\nAlso note fifa has photo URLs etc. — skip.\n\nNow the derby table. Classic Brazilian derbies to include:\n- Fla–Flu: Flamengo vs Fluminense\n- Clássico dos Milhões: Flamengo vs Vasco\n- Gre-nal: Grêmio vs Internacional\n- Majestoso: Corinthians vs São Paulo\n- Choque-Rei: São Paulo vs Santos\n- Clássico Alvinegro? (Santos vs Corinthians — \"Clássico da Vila\"/Brasileirão rivalries) — Santos vs Corinthians is \"Clássico Alvinegro\"? Hmm. Botafogo vs Flamengo = \"Clássico da Rivalidade\"; Vasco vs Fluminense = \"Clássico dos Gigantes\"? I'll include a reasonable set:\n  - Flamengo–Fluminense: Fla-Flu\n  - Flamengo–Vasco: Clássico dos Milhões\n  - Fluminense–Vasco: Clássico dos Gigantes? Hmm... Actually \"Clássico dos Gigantes\" isn't standard; I'll skip or include as some do. Let me be careful — I'll include widely-known ones:\n  - Botafogo–Flamengo: Clássico da Rivalidade\n  - Botafogo–Fluminense: Clássico Vovô (oldest derby in Brazil)\n  - Vasco–Botafogo: Clássico dos Gigantes? Hmm — some call it that. Skip risky ones? A derby list doesn't need to be exhaustive; each entry just needs the name. I'll include:\n    - Fla-Flu (Flamengo × Fluminense)\n    - Clássico dos Milhões (Flamengo × Vasco)\n    - Clássico Vovô (Botafogo × Fluminense)\n    - Clássico da Rivalidade (Botafogo × Flamengo)\n    - Gre-nal (Grêmio × Internacional)\n    - Clássico Majestoso (Corinthians × São Paulo)\n    - Choque-Rei (São Paulo × Santos)\n    - Derby Paulista / Clássico da Saudade? Palmeiras × Santos = \"Clássico da Saudade\"; Palmeiras × Corinthians = \"O Derby\" (Derby Paulista); Palmeiras × São Paulo = \"Choque Rei\"? No — Choque-Rei is São Paulo vs Santos? Hmm, actually \"Choque-Rei\" is São Paulo vs Santos; \"Choque Monarca\"? Let me get this right:\n      - Palmeiras × Corinthians: \"O Derby\" (Derby Paulista) ✓\n      - Corinthians × São Paulo: \"Majestoso\" ✓\n      - São Paulo × Santos: \"Choque-Rei\" ✓ (also \"Clássico dos Reis\"? no)\n      - Palmeiras × Santos: \"Clássico da Saudade\" ✓\n      - Palmeiras × São Paulo: \"Choque Monarca\"? No — that's not common. It's \"Clássico do Vale do Anhangabaú\"? Rare. Skip.\n    - Atlético-MG × Cruzeiro: \"Clássico Mineiro\" (Atletibão? — the derby is called \"Clássico Mineiro\" or \"Atlético-Cruzeiro\") ✓\n    - Atlético-MG × América-MG: \"Clássico das Multidões\"? Skip? Include maybe.\n    - Bahia × Vitória: \"Ba-Vi\" ✓\n    - Sport × Náutico: \"Clássico dos Clássicos\" ✓\n    - Sport × Santa Cruz: \"Clássico das Multidões\" ✓\n    - Náutico × Santa Cruz: \"Clássico dos Emoções\"? include\n    - Coritiba × Athletico-PR: \"Atléti-tiba\"? — Coritiba vs Atlético-PR is \"Atletiba\" ✓\n    - Athletico-PR × Paraná: \"Paratiba\"? skip? It exists (\"Paratiba\" — Atlético-PR vs Paraná Clube). Include.\n    - Coritiba × Paraná: \"Coritibanão\"? \"Copa Paratiba\"? skip.\n    - Ceará × Fortaleza: \"Clássico-Rei\" ✓\n    - Remo × Paysandu: \"Re-Pa\" ✓\n    - CSA × CRB: \"Clássico das Multidões Alagoanas\"? CRB vs CSA is \"Clássico das Multidões\"... Hmm. I'll include as \"Clássico Alagoano (CRB × CSA)\". Fine.\n    - Avaí × Figueirense: \"Clássico de Florianópolis\" (\"Clássico Catarinense\"?). Include as \"O Clássico Catarinense\"? Avaí–Figueirense = \"Clássico de Florianópolis\" ✓.\n    - Goiás × Vila Nova: \"Clássico do Cerrado\"? — Goiás vs Vila Nova is \"Clássico Goiano\" / \"Derby do Cerrado\" ✓.\n\nThat's a good set. Now the \"show me all derbies in 2023\" query needs matches between these pairs in a season.\n\n**Data loading performance**: 42k rows total — trivial to load in memory (<1s). Standings and stats computed on the fly are fine (<5s). Cache the loaded dataset at module level (lru_cache or singleton).\n\n**Dedup strategy for cross-source queries**: When searching matches \"by team\" across all sources, duplicates would show up (same match in 2-3 files). I'll dedupe display results by (date, home_key, away_key, score) keeping the first source. For standings: use a canonical source per competition-season to avoid double-count:\n- Competition \"Serie A\" (Brasileirão): canonical source per season: novo for 2003-2011; Brasileirao_Matches for 2012-2022; BR-Football for 2023 (only BRFB has 2023 Serie A!). Actually BRFB 2023 Serie A has 377 matches (some missing). Hmm — but \"who won the 2023 Brasileirão\" — Palmeiras won. Computing from 377 of 380 matches — close enough, likely still Palmeiras on top. That's fine, mark it as \"calculated from available matches\".\n  \n  Wait, actually — should standings for Serie A unify all sources with dedup? For 2012-2019 both Brasileirao_Matches and novo have full 380; dedup by (date, teams, score) — but overlap check showed 0 exact matches because dates might differ between files? Let me check: novo \"Data\" for 2015 Flamengo games vs Brasileirao_Matches datetime. Both should have real dates. My earlier check compared raw date strings — \"29/03/2003\" vs \"2015-...\". For 2015 both would be ISO from datetime vs DD/MM from novo. Let me parse properly and recheck overlap. If dates agree, dedup by (date, home, away) works. If not, dedup by (season, round, home, away).\n\nFor simplicity and determinism, I'll take the \"canonical source per season\" approach for standings/team stats:\n- For each competition, sources have priority order; for a given season, use the highest-priority source that covers that season (has ≥ 90% of the max match count for that season across sources... too complex). Simpler: fixed priority per competition with season coverage check:\n  - \"Serie A\": [Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022), novo (2003-2019), BR-Football (2014-2023)] → pick first that has matches for the season.\n  - So 2003-2011 → novo; 2012-2022 → Brasileirao_Matches; 2023 → BR-Football.\n  - \"Copa do Brasil\": [Brazilian_Cup_Matches (2012-2021), BR-Football (2014-2023)] → 2012-2021 → Brazilian_Cup; 2022-2023 → BRFB.\n  - \"Libertadores\": [Libertadores_Matches (2013-2022)].\n  - \"Serie B\"/\"Serie C\": [BR-Football].\n\nBut hmm — for standings the double-round-robin logic: Serie A/B are league format (points table meaningful). Copa do Brasil and Libertadores are cups (knockout + group stage) — a \"standings\" table for cups isn't a league table. The spec asks \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\" (league — points) and \"Show the 2018 Copa Libertadores bracket\" (cup — bracket). So:\n- `standings` tool: for league competitions (Serie A, Serie B, Serie C), compute points table.\n- For cups: Libertadores — I could compute group-stage tables by group, but the data doesn't have groups. Hmm. For \"who won the Libertadores 2019?\" I can find the final match (stage == \"final\") and report the winner (two-legged: aggregate). Let me implement `competition_final(competition, season)`: for Libertadores find final(s) by stage; for Copa do Brasil by highest round number of the season; aggregate two-legged finals.\n- \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" — bottom 4 of Serie A standings.\n- \"2018 Copa Libertadores bracket\" — knockout stage matches: round of 16, quarterfinals, semifinals, final — list pairings with aggregates. I can implement a simple bracket summary from knockout matches. That might be complex; I'll implement a best-effort \"bracket\" listing knockout ties with aggregate scores.\n\n**MCP tools final list** (name, params):\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, from_date=None, to_date=None, stage=None, limit=20)` — returns formatted match list + count. Covers: by team (either/home/away — hmm \"home or either\": add `team_side` param? Better: `team_side: \"any\"|\"home\"|\"away\"`), date range, competition, season, stage/round.\n2. `last_match_between(team_a, team_b)` — most recent + score.\n3. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)` — full H2H record + recent matches.\n4. `team_stats(team, competition=None, season=None, venue=\"all\")` — W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate; venue home/away/all.\n5. `best_team_records(competition=None, season=None, venue=\"all\", min_matches=10, limit=5)` — best home record / best away record / overall.\n6. `team_competitions(team)` — which competitions a team played in (across all files), with match counts and seasons.\n7. `search_players(name=None, club=None, nationality=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20)` — player search.\n8. `top_players(club=None, nationality=None, position=None, limit=10)` — sorted by Overall.\n9. `player_clubs_summary(nationality=\"Brazil\")` — \"Brazilian players at Brazilian clubs: Flamengo: 8 players (avg rating 74)\" style — aggregated by club. Call it `players_by_club`.\n10. `standings(competition=\"Serie A\", season=2019)` — full points table (league comps) or best-effort for cups (maybe error with hint). Relegation info included (bottom 4)? I'll add `relegated` flag in standings output for Serie A/B, or separate tool `relegation`. Include in standings text: \"Relegation zone: ...\". Good.\n11. `competition_champion(competition, season)` — league: top of table; cup: final winner (Libertadores final stage / Copa do Brasil last round aggregate).\n12. `competition_bracket(competition, season)` — knockout bracket for cups (Libertadores stages; Copa do Brasil rounds 5+? Copa do Brasil round numbers: 1-8 where 8 = final. Actually rounds: 1=first round... 7=semifinal, 8=final).\n13. `competition_info()` — list all competitions, seasons, match counts, sources.\n14. `average_goals(competition=None, season=None, team=None)` — avg goals per match, home win/draw/away win rates.\n15. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, team=None, limit=5)` — largest margins.\n16. `derbies(season=None, limit=50)` — all derby matches with derby names (or `list_derbies()` to enumerate known derbies).\n17. `team_profile(team)` — combined: aliases used, stats overview, competitions, top-rated players at that club (cross-file!). This covers \"cross-file queries\" explicitly (player + match data).\n\nThat's plenty. Plus a `help`/`list_tools`? MCP protocol lists tools anyway. Maybe add `data_overview` = competition_info. Fine.\n\nNatural language → tool mapping is the LLM's job (MCP server connected to an LLM). Good.\n\n**Response format**: spec shows formatted text answers. I'll return strings (MCP tools can return str content). Some structured info via JSON-ish lines. I'll return plain text formatted like the examples. Maybe both: a compact human-readable text. MCP tools can also return structured content — mcp 2.x with pydantic structured output. Simpler: return strings.\n\n**BDD tests**: pytest, GWT style. I'll write classes per feature with scenario docstrings in Gherkin and `test_given_*_when_*_then_*` method names. Cover:\n- Match queries: search between two teams; by team+season; by competition; date range; last match; stage (finals).\n- Team queries: team stats home record; best home record; goals scored most in season; team competitions.\n- Player queries: search by name (Neymar); Brazilian players; players at Brazilian club (Grêmio); top players; players by club aggregation.\n- Competition: standings 2019 (Flamengo champion 90 pts — assert from data); standings 2003 from novo; relegated 2020 (bottom of table); Libertadores 2019 final (Flamengo won); Copa do Brasil champion by year; bracket.\n- Statistics: average goals; biggest wins; home vs away; head-to-head Fla-Flu counts.\n- Normalization: team name variants map to same key; date parsing multiple formats; UTF-8 names.\n- MCP server: tool listing via MCPServer API, call_tool for a few tools, verify response strings.\n\nFor server tests with mcp 2.x: `await server.call_tool(\"search_matches\", {...})` returns CallToolResult. Need asyncio — pytest without pytest-asyncio... I can use `asyncio.run()` inside sync tests. Fine.\n\nLet me check call_tool return shape and list_tools in this version. Also verify the `tool` decorator usage: in mcp 2.x, is it `@mcp.tool` or `@mcp.tool()`? Signature shows `tool(self, name=None, title=None, description=None, ...)` returns decorator. So both `@mcp.tool` and `@mcp.tool()` work? Let me check source. Also how does it infer tool name/description from function? Let me read the mcpserver source quickly for tool() and how to run stdio server: `run_stdio_async()` / `mcp.run()`. And the `mcp.server.stdio` for stdio_server context. In 2.x there's `run_stdio_async`. Let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d65c520001vVTBdsvlcJUDq5","sessionID":"ses_fa29f8b64ffekf1eHikDs909KG","messageID":"msg_05d64bc1f0016ccvl8HPayVGa6"}}
