"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nd = defaultdict(lambda: defaultdict(int))\nwith open('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        d[row['season']][row['round']] += 1\nfor s in sorted(d):\n    print(s, dict(sorted(d[s].items())))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788273478568,"end":1788273478574}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Important discoveries:\n1. Copa do Brasil team names in this file use a DIFFERENT format: \"Atlético Paranaense - PR\", \"Flamengo - RJ\" (space-hyphen-space separator, with accents). So normalization: \" - RJ\" suffix with spaces. My norm must handle \" - \" separators.\n2. Round 8 = final (2-legged). 2013: Flamengo won (agg 3-1 vs Atlético Paranaense). 2014: Atlético-MG (agg 3-2 over Cruzeiro). 2015: Palmeiras (agg 3-2 over Santos — 1-0 loss + 2-1 win → agg 2-2?? wait: Santos 1-0 Palmeiras, then Palmeiras 2-1 Santos → agg 2-2. Palmeiras won on penalties! So 2015 final aggregate was tied). 2016: round 8 missing (finals: Grêmio beat Atlético-MG? No — 2016 Copa do Brasil champion was Grêmio, beating Atlético Mineiro. r7 shows Atlético-MG vs Grêmio semifinal; no r8 → final data missing for 2016). 2017: Cruzeiro (agg 2-1). 2018: Cruzeiro (agg 3-1). 2019: Athletico Paranaense (agg 3-1). 2020: Palmeiras (agg 3-1). 2021: data missing? rounds show no 2021 r7/r8? 2021 season has 108 matches, no round 8. So champion resolution: last available round; if final round missing, report \"final not in dataset\".\n\nSo for cup champion: take the max round in that season, aggregate goals across the (typically 2) matches between the two teams; if aggregate tied → \"decided on penalties (not in dataset)\" and note both finalists. That's honest and accurate. For 2015 I can note Palmeiras won on penalties? The data alone shows a tie — I'll report the tie + penalties note. Good.\n\nAlso note: \"Athletico Paranaense - PR\" (2019+) vs \"Atlético Paranaense - PR\" (2013) — same club, different spelling eras. Alias needed.\n\nAlso \"America - MG\" (with space-hyphen-space) in Copa file vs \"America-MG\" in Brasileirao file. Normalization handles by tokenizing on non-alphanumerics.\n\nNow Brasileirao_Matches.csv: names like \"Atletico-MG\" (no accent). novo: \"Athletico-PR\"? Let me check novo's team names quickly — it showed \"Athletico-PR\" in row 2 of head. And \"Grêmio\" with accent. OK — accents handled by NFKD.\n\nNow, BR-Football 2023 Serie A standings — let me sanity check Palmeiras is #1. Also check total match counts per season to handle \"missing data\" gracefully.\n\nActually, I've explored enough. Let me also quickly confirm: does BR-Football dataset have \"Flamengo\" and \"Palmeiras\" (yes, saw Flamengo in first row, Palmeiras in list). For 2023 Serie A: 377 matches — champion from calc. Let me verify quickly, then start writing code.\n\nAlso need FIFA \"Vitória\" vs \"Vitória Guimarães\": club search for \"Vitória\" must match exact club name, not substring... Actually for player club search I'll use substring match but that's the documented behavior (\"Filter FIFA data by Club containing 'Flamengo'\"). For players_by_club aggregation with Brazilian clubs, I'll map FIFA club names through the same team-normalization so \"Vitória\" → \"vitoria\" and \"Vitória Guimarães\" → \"vitoria guimaraes\" (distinct keys — good). Brazilian club detection for FIFA clubs: I'll define a set of known Brazilian club canonical keys and check if normalized FIFA club is in the set of known Brazilian teams from match data... but \"Santos Laguna\" would normalize to \"santos laguna\" ≠ \"santos\" — good, exact key match. But \"Santos\" (Brazil) vs \"Santos Laguna\": distinct keys. What about FIFA \"Paraná\" matching match-data \"Paraná\"? Good. FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" → alias → \"atletico mg\". FIFA \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → needs alias → \"ceara\". FIFA \"Sport Club