{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788273604880,"sessionID":"ses_fa29f8b64ffekf1eHikDs909KG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_766e2d4ee2594d5d9a97720f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, unicodedata\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nUF = {'SP','RJ','MG','RS','PR','SC','PE','CE','BA','GO','AL','MT','MS','PA','RN','PB','SE','PI','MA','TO','RO','AC','AM','AP','RR','DF','ES'}\n\ndef slug(s):\n    s = unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', s)\n    s = ''.join(c for c in s if not unicodedata.combining(c)).lower()\n    import re\n    m = re.findall(r'\\(([^)]*)\\)', s)\n    s = re.sub(r'\\([^)]*\\)', ' ', s)\n    for x in m:\n        x = x.strip()\n        if x and len(x.split()) == 1 and len(x) <= 4:\n            s += ' ' + x\n    s = ''.join(c if c.isalnum() else ' ' for c in s)\n    return ' '.join(s.split())\n\nBASES = {'palmeiras','flamengo','corinthians','santos','sao paulo','sport','bahia','ceara','fortaleza','vitoria','cruzeiro','coritiba','parana','chapecoense','avai','figueirense','criciuma','goias','gremio','internacional','fluminense','guarani','portuguesa','juventude','caxias','nautico','remo','paysandu','sampaio correa','csa','crb','abc','vasco','vila nova'}\ndef key(t):\n    s = slug(t)\n    toks = s.split()\n    if toks and toks[-1] in UF:\n        base = ' '.join(toks[:-1])\n        if base in BASES: return base\n    return s\n\ndef load(path, hcol, acol, hg, ag, season, dcol, dparse):\n    rows = []\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n            try:\n                hgi, agi = int(float(row[hg])), int(float(row[ag]))\n            except (ValueError, TypeError): continue\n            rows.append((dparse(row[dcol]), key(row[hcol]), key(row[acol]), hgi, agi, int(row[season])))\n    return rows\n\nimport re\ndef iso(d): return d[:10]\ndef brd(d):\n    dd, mm, yy = d.split('/'); return f\"{yy}-{mm}-{dd}\"\n\na = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv','home_team','away_team','home_goal','away_goal','season','datetime', iso)\nb = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','Equipe_mandante','Equipe_visitante','Gols_mandante','Gols_visitante','Ano','Data', brd)\nf = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','home','away','home_goal','away_goal','date','date', lambda d: d[:10])\n\n# score agreement between a and b on (season, home, away)\nma = {(s,h,aw):(hg,ag) for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in a}\nmb = {(s,h,aw):(hg,ag) for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in b}\ncommon = set(ma) & set(mb)\nagree = sum(1 for k in common if ma[k]==mb[k])\nprint(f\"novo vs brfile common fixtures: {len(common)}, score agreement: {agree}\")\n\ndef table(rows, year):\n    pts = {}\n    for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in rows:\n        if s != year: continue\n        for t in (h,aw): pts.setdefault(t, [0,0,0,0,0,0])\n        if hg>ag: pts[h][0]+=3; pts[h][1]+=1; pts[aw][3]+=1\n        elif hg<ag: pts[aw][0]+=3; pts[aw][1]+=1; pts[h][3]+=1\n        else: pts[h][0]+=1; pts[aw][0]+=1; pts[h][2]+=1; pts[aw][2]+=1\n        pts[h][4]+=hg; pts[h][5]+=ag; pts[aw][4]+=ag; pts[aw][5]+=hg\n    return sorted(pts.items(), key=lambda kv:(-kv[1][0], -(kv[1][4]-kv[1][5]), -kv[1][4]))\n\n# 2023 Serie A from BRFB\nserieA23 = [(d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in f if s==2023]\nprint(\"2023 Serie A matches:\", len(serieA23))\nfor t,v in table(serieA23, 2023)[:4]: print(\" 23:\", t, v)\n# 2020 bottom from brfile\nfor t,v in table(a, 2020)[-4:]: print(\" 2020 bottom:\", t, v)\nEOF"},"output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 45, in <module>\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 35, in load\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: '2023-09-24'\n","metadata":{"output":"Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 45, in <module>\n  File \"<stdin>\", line 35, in load\nValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: '2023-09-24'\n","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, unicodedata\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nUF = {'SP','RJ','MG','RS','PR','SC','PE','CE','BA','GO','AL','MT','MS','PA','RN','PB','SE','PI','MA','TO','RO','AC','AM','AP','RR','DF','ES'}\n\ndef slug(s):\n    s = unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', s)\n    s = ''.join(c for c in s if not unicodedata.combining(c)).lower()\n    import re\n    m = re.findall(r'\\(([^)]*)\\)', s)\n    s = re.sub(r'\\([^)]*\\)', ' ', s)\n    for x in m:\n        x = x.strip()\n        if x and len(x.split()) == 1 and len(x) <= 4:\n            s += ' ' + x\n    s = ''.join(c if c.isalnum() else ' ' for c in s)\n    return ' '.join(s.split())\n\nBASES = {'palmeiras','flamengo','corinthians','santos','sao paulo','sport','bahia','ceara','fortaleza','vitoria','cruzeiro','coritiba','parana','chapecoense','avai','figueirense','criciuma','goias','gremio','internacional','fluminense','guarani','portuguesa','juventude','caxias','nautico','remo','paysandu','sampaio correa','csa','crb','abc','vasco','vila nova'}\ndef key(t):\n    s = slug(t)\n    toks = s.split()\n    if toks and toks[-1] in UF:\n        base = ' '.join(toks[:-1])\n        if base in BASES: return base\n    return s\n\ndef load(path, hcol, acol, hg, ag, season, dcol, dparse):\n    rows = []\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n            try:\n                hgi, agi = int(float(row[hg])), int(float(row[ag]))\n            except (ValueError, TypeError): continue\n            rows.append((dparse(row[dcol]), key(row[hcol]), key(row[acol]), hgi, agi, int(row[season])))\n    return rows\n\nimport re\ndef iso(d): return d[:10]\ndef brd(d):\n    dd, mm, yy = d.split('/'); return f\"{yy}-{mm}-{dd}\"\n\na = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv','home_team','away_team','home_goal','away_goal','season','datetime', iso)\nb = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv','Equipe_mandante','Equipe_visitante','Gols_mandante','Gols_visitante','Ano','Data', brd)\nf = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','home','away','home_goal','away_goal','date','date', lambda d: d[:10])\n\n# score agreement between a and b on (season, home, away)\nma = {(s,h,aw):(hg,ag) for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in a}\nmb = {(s,h,aw):(hg,ag) for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in b}\ncommon = set(ma) & set(mb)\nagree = sum(1 for k in common if ma[k]==mb[k])\nprint(f\"novo vs brfile common fixtures: {len(common)}, score agreement: {agree}\")\n\ndef table(rows, year):\n    pts = {}\n    for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in rows:\n        if s != year: continue\n        for t in (h,aw): pts.setdefault(t, [0,0,0,0,0,0])\n        if hg>ag: pts[h][0]+=3; pts[h][1]+=1; pts[aw][3]+=1\n        elif hg<ag: pts[aw][0]+=3; pts[aw][1]+=1; pts[h][3]+=1\n        else: pts[h][0]+=1; pts[aw][0]+=1; pts[h][2]+=1; pts[aw][2]+=1\n        pts[h][4]+=hg; pts[h][5]+=ag; pts[aw][4]+=ag; pts[aw][5]+=hg\n    return sorted(pts.items(), key=lambda kv:(-kv[1][0], -(kv[1][4]-kv[1][5]), -kv[1][4]))\n\n# 2023 Serie A from BRFB\nserieA23 = [(d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in f if s==2023]\nprint(\"2023 Serie A matches:\", len(serieA23))\nfor t,v in table(serieA23, 2023)[:4]: print(\" 23:\", t, v)\n# 2020 bottom from brfile\nfor t,v in table(a, 2020)[-4:]: print(\" 2020 bottom:\", t, v)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788273604876,"end":1788273604878}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Copa do Brasil rounds by season are inconsistent (formats changed over years; data partial). Max round per season: 2012:6, 2013-2015:8, 2016:7, 2017-2020:8, 2021:4. And the counts don't always match official formats (data is partial). So: stage mapping should be dynamic per season: last round of the season = \"final\", second last = \"semifinal\". For 2016 (max 7): round 7 = final? But 2016 final was Grêmio vs Atlético-MG... wait no, 2016 Copa do Brasil final was Grêmio × Atlético-MG — and the r7 matches show \"Atlético - MG 1-3 Grêmio - RS\" + \"Grêmio - RS 1-1 Atlético - MG\" — those ARE the 2016 final legs! (agg 4-2 Grêmio). So in 2016, round 7 = final. Confirms: dynamic last-round = final works.\n\nHmm, but careful: for a query \"find all Copa do Brasil finals\" across seasons, using per-season max round is right. For 2021 (max 4) — 2021 Copa do Brasil actually went to a final (Atlético-MG champion) but data only has up to round 4 (32 matches = round of 32?). So \"final not in dataset for 2021\" — the champion tool must report that honestly: \"2021 Copa do Brasil: data only covers up to Round 4; final not in dataset (actual champion: not determinable from data)\". Good.\n\nDesign decision for stage labels in Copa loader: compute per-season max round at load time → label matches: last round → \"final\", second-last → \"semifinal\", third-last → \"quarterfinal\". Hmm, is third-last quarterfinal always? 2013: rounds 1..8 → 8=final, 7=SF, 6=QF ✓. 2012: max 6 → 6=final, 5=SF, 4=QF ✓ (2012 format: 6 rounds, final = Palmeiras vs Coritiba ✓). 2016: max 7 → 7=final ✓ (Grêmio × Atlético-MG ✓), 6=SF, 5=QF ✓. 2017-2020: 8 rounds ✓. 2021: max 4 → 4=final?? NO — 2021 data stops at round 4 which is NOT the final (actual format had 7 rounds; data incomplete). Labeling round 4 as \"final\" would be wrong!\n\nHmm. So dynamic labeling breaks for incomplete seasons. How to distinguish? For two-legged finals: the final round should have ≤2 matches between exactly 2 teams. 2021 round 4: 16 matches — clearly not a final. Heuristic: last round = \"final\" only if that round has ≤ 2 matches; second-last = \"semifinal\" if it has ≤ 4 matches between ≤4 teams... 2021: round 4 has 16 matches → not final; round 3 has 32 → not SF. So 2021 gets no final label — honest. 2012: round 6 has 2 matches (2 teams) → final ✓. 2016: round 7 = 2 matches → final ✓. 2013-2015, 2017-2020: round 8 = 2 matches → final ✓. 2020 special: r8 has 2 matches (Grêmio 0-1 Palmeiras; Palmeiras 2-0 Grêmio) ✓.\n\nAlso single-legged finals would have 1 match — covered by ≤2.\n\nSemifinal: second-to-last round with ≤4 matches → \"semifinal\" (2012 r5: 4 matches ✓; 2013 r7: 4 ✓; 2016 r6: 4 ✓; 2021 r3: 32 ✗ no label). Quarterfinal: ≤8 matches. Round of 16: ≤16. Otherwise label \"round N\".\n\nWait — 2013 r6: 8 matches → QF ✓. 2017 r5: 16 → round of 16 ✓. Labels for early rounds just stay \"round N\". Good.\n\nNow — Copa champion tool: use matches labeled \"final\"; aggregate two legs; if tied → \"decided on penalties (not in dataset)\". Also note when season's final is missing from data.\n\nLibertadores champion: stage==\"final\" matches; aggregate legs (2019 single leg 2 matches? no, 2019 had ONE final match: 2019-11-23 Flamengo 2-1 River Plate. Earlier finals 2 legs). Aggregate logic: sum goals per team across legs; if tied (2013: 2-2) → penalties note. 2013 actual champion Atlético-MG — I can't determine from data; report tie + note. That's the honest answer. \n\nActually for 2013, hmm, aggregate: Olimpia 2-0 Atlético-MG; Atlético-MG 2-0 Olimpia. Agg 2-2. Champion on penalties: Atlético-MG. Data doesn't have penalties. Report: \"Tied 2-2 on aggregate (Atlético-MG won on penalties — penalty data not in dataset)\". Should I hardcode actual champions as a fallback? NO — spec says \"calculated from match results\". Honest reporting is better. But hmm, the \" champion\" for 2015 Copa do Brasil: Palmeiras 2-2 Santos agg... 2015: Santos 1-0 Palmeiras; Palmeiras 2-1 Santos → agg 2-2 → penalties → Palmeiras. Report tie+note. Fine.\n\nNow — what about league champion accuracy check: 2023 Serie A from BRFB (377/380 matches): Palmeiras? Let me verify in the test exploration. Also relegated 2020 (from Brasileirao_Matches 2020, full 380): bottom 4 = Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, Goiás (historical fact) — test will assert data-derived values; I'll compute expected from data at test-authoring time and hardcode assertions (that's fine — they're facts).\n\nLet me now verify 2023 Serie A standings + 2020 bottom 4, then check novo/Brasileirao round agreement (not needed), then WRITE THE CODE.\n\nAlso: performance — loading 42k CSV rows + FIFA 18k rows on import. FIFA players: 18k rows × ~80 cols — fine. Load lazily? Server startup loads all — ~1-2s. Fine. For tests, a module-level cached dataset (load once, reuse across tests via fixture).\n\nProject structure (flat-ish, in current dir):\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer/\n  __init__.py\n  models.py        # Match, Player, TeamRecord dataclasses\n  normalize.py     # accent stripping, team key normalization, aliases, display names, date parsing\n  data.py          # load all CSVs → Dataset (matches, players, indexes); competition registry\n  query.py         # all query functions returning structured results\n  format.py?       # maybe merge into query/server — skip, format in server.py\n  server.py        # MCPServer + tool definitions (formatted string outputs)\nmain.py            # entry: python main.py [--transport stdio]\ntests/\n  __init__.py? (no)\n  conftest.py      # dataset fixture\n  test_match_queries.py      (BDD feature: Match Queries)\n  test_team_queries.py       (BDD feature: Team Queries)\n  test_player_queries.py     (BDD feature: Player Queries)\n  test_competition_queries.py(BDD feature: Competition Queries)\n  test_statistics.py         (BDD feature: Statistical Analysis)\n  test_normalization.py      (Data quality: name variations, date formats, UTF-8)\n  test_mcp_server.py         (BDD feature: MCP Server Tools)\nrequirements.txt\npyproject.toml (optional; keep simple: requirements.txt + pytest.ini maybe)\nREADME.md (update at end)\n```\n\nWait — \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" Hmm — \"current directory\" means the repo root here; a package subdir is still \"in the current directory\" (working tree). But to be safe and simple, maybe put modules flat in root? \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — I think a package subdirectory is fine and idiomatic; but the instruction might literally mean root-level files. Hmm. A python package subdir `brazilian_soccer/` keeps it clean, still within the working directory. I'll do that — it's the standard interpretation (don't write to other directories). Tests in `tests/`.\n\nLet me also add `pytest.ini` or config in pyproject for test discovery, plus `requirements.txt` (mcp only; stdlib csv otherwise).\n\nNow, one more verification round: 2023 Serie A standings from BRFB, 2020 relegations, and fifa Age/Overall parsing (strings), and check heights like \"5'7\" (feet). Fine.\n\nThen write:\n\n**normalize.py**:\n```python\nimport re, unicodedata\n\nBRAZILIAN_STATES = {...2-letter...}\n\nTEAM_ALIASES = {\n    \"atletico mineiro\": \"atletico mg\",\n    \"athletico paranaense\": \"atletico pr\",\n    \"atletico paranaense\": \"atletico pr\",\n    \"sport club corinthians paulista\": \"corinthians\",\n    \"sport club do recife\": \"sport\",\n    \"sport recife\": \"sport\",\n    \"ceara sporting club\": \"ceara\",\n    \"ceara sc\": \"ceara\",\n    \"clube do remo\": \"remo\",\n    \"nautico capibaribe\": \"nautico\",\n    \"vasco da gama\": \"vasco\",\n    \"boavista sport club\": \"boavista rj\",   # from \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo ...)\" after paren-strip → \"boavista sport club\"\n    \"cs alagoano\": \"csa\",\n    \"cs sergipe\": \"sergipe\",\n    \"clube sport maritimo\"?? no that's Portuguese (Marítimo) — leave.\n    \"gremio rs\": \"gremio\",\n    \"internacional rs\": \"internacional\",\n    \"coritiba pr\" → handled by suffix strip? \"Coritiba PR\" → strip \"pr\" suffix → \"coritiba\" ✓. But \"Internacional RS\" → strip \"rs\" → \"internacional\" ✓. \"Gremio RS\" → \"gremio\" ✓. \"America MG\" → \"america mg\"?? WAIT — stripping the state suffix would turn \"America MG\" into \"america\" — COLLISION with America RN!\n```\n\nSTOP. Critical design decision: do NOT strip state suffixes blindly. The state suffix must be KEPT as part of the key: \"atletico mg\", \"atletico pr\", \"america mg\", \"america rn\". Then alias un-suffixed FULL names to suffixed keys: \"atletico mineiro\" → \"atletico mg\", \"atletico paranaense\" → \"atletico pr\".\n\nBut then \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras sp\" vs novo \"Palmeiras\" → \"palmeiras\" — MISMATCH. Ugh. The suffix is sometimes present, sometimes not.\n\nResolution: strip the state suffix ONLY when the resulting base is NOT ambiguous among known team bases. I.e., build the set of all base names (suffix-stripped). If a base is unique (only one club has that base across all data), drop the suffix → key = base. If ambiguous (atletico, america, botafogo, fluminense, vitoria, nacional, santos? no santos laguna base = \"santos laguna\" ≠ \"santos\"), keep suffix as disambiguator.\n\nAmbiguous bases in our data (multiple clubs share base): \n- atletico: atletico mg, atletico pr, atletico go, atletico goianiense(→ atletico go?)... \n- america: america mg, america rn\n- botafogo: botafogo rj, botafogo pb, botafogo sp\n- fluminense: fluminense rj, fluminense de feira (base \"fluminense de feira\" ≠ \"fluminense\"), fluminense pi. Hmm — \"Fluminense De Feira\" base after strip = \"fluminense de feira\" (no UF suffix; \"De Feira\" isn't UF) → distinct base already. \"Fluminense RJ\" → base \"fluminense\"; \"Fluminense PI\" → base \"fluminense\". So base \"fluminense\" maps to {fluminense rj, fluminense pi} → ambiguous → keep suffix. But FIFA \"Fluminense\" and Brasileirao \"Fluminense-RJ\" → both become \"fluminense rj\"? The bare \"Fluminense\" (no suffix) — which club? Ambiguous base → can't resolve... but realistically Fluminense = the RJ giant.\n\nPragmatic solution: curated CANONICAL table for all significant clubs (the ~60 clubs in Serie A/B 2012-2023 + FIFA Brazilian clubs + common Libertadores Brazilian clubs), mapping every observed variant → canonical key. Algorithmic normalization as fallback for obscure Serie D clubs (exact normalized string match; minor duplicates there are acceptable).\n\nThis is the robust path. Let me generate the full variant list programmatically and write the alias table. The normalization pipeline:\n\n```\ndef team_key(raw):\n    s = strip_accents(raw.lower())\n    s = re.sub(r'\\(.*?\\)', ' ', s)  # drop parenthetical... but Nacional (URU) vs Nacional (PAR)!\n```\n\nHmm — parentheticals distinguish foreign clubs. Transform \"(URU)\" → \" uru\": re.sub(r'\\((.*?)\\)', r' \\1', s). Then \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional uru\" ✓ distinct from \"nacional par\" ✓.\n\nThen tokens on non-alphanumeric → collapse spaces. Then strip state suffix token IF last token is UF AND base-without-it is unambiguous? No wait — I decided: canonical table + suffix-KEEP.\n\nLet me define: \n1. `slug(raw)` = lowercase, NFKD de-accent, parenthetical → token, non-alnum → space, collapse, strip.\n2. Alias map `ALIASES: slug → canonical_key`. Canonical keys also as slugs, e.g. \"atletico mg\".\n3. `team_key(raw)`: s = slug(raw); if s in ALIASES → ALIASES[s]; else if s ends with UF token: base = s minus UF; if base in ALIASES→? no...\n\nOK here's the cleanest scheme — TWO-STAGE with explicit canonical keys:\n\nCanonical keys are themselves slugs WITH state suffix for clubs that need it: \"atletico mg\", \"atletico pr\", \"america mg\", \"america rn\", \"botafogo rj\", \"botafogo sp\", \"botafogo pb\", \"fluminense\", \"vasco\", \"santos\", \"palmeiras\", \"flamengo\", \"corinthians\", \"sao paulo\", \"gremio\", \"internacional\", \"sport\", \"ceara\", \"fortaleza\", \"bahia\", \"vitoria\", \"cruzeiro\", \"coritiba\", \"parana\", \"chapecoense\", \"avai\", \"figueirense\", \"criciuma\", \"goias\", \"vila nova\", \"atletico goianiense\"/\"atletico go\"...\n\nRules to derive variant → canonical:\n- ALIAS table for known full names: \"atletico mineiro\"→\"atletico mg\", \"athletico paranaense\"/\"atletico paranaense\"→\"atletico pr\", \"atletico goianiense\"→\"atletico go\", \"sport club do recife\"/\"sport recife\"→\"sport\", \"ceara sporting club\"→\"ceara\", \"clube do remo\"→\"remo\", \"nautico capibaribe\"→\"nautico\", \"vasco da gama\"→\"vasco\", \"cs alagoano\"→\"csa\", \"cs sergipe\"→\"sergipe\", \"boavista sport club\"→\"boavista\", \"boavista sc saquarema\"→\"boavista\", \"gremio rs\"→\"gremio\", \"internacional rs\"→\"internacional\", \"fluminense de feira\"→\"fluminense de feira\" (own key), \"sao jose poa\"?? skip, etc.\n- SUFFIX-DROP table: drop trailing UF token when the base is a KNOWN unambiguous club: bases like palmeiras, flamengo, corinthians, santos, sao paulo, vasco, sport, bahia, ceara, fortaleza, vitoria?? — \"vitoria\" bare: FIFA \"Vitória\" (BA) and match data \"Vitoria-BA\"/\"Vitoria ES\"/\"Vitoria da Conquista\". Base \"vitoria\" appears as {vitoria ba, vitoria es} → ambiguous... but \"EC Vitoria\"/\"Vitoria\" in BRFB = Vitória BA. And \"Vitoria EC\" → vitoria ba. Hmm. For bare \"vitoria\": the overwhelming majority = Vitória-BA. I'll alias bare \"vitoria\" → \"vitoria ba\" and keep \"vitoria es\" distinct (it always appears WITH suffix). Bare occurrences: FIFA \"Vitória\" (20 players, = BA ✓), BRFB \"Vitoria\"/\"Vitoria EC\" (BA ✓). OK.\n  \nSo the algorithm:\n```\ndef team_key(raw):\n    s = slug(raw)  # with parenthetical→token\n    if s in ALIASES: return ALIASES[s]\n    toks = s.split()\n    if toks and toks[-1] in UF_CODES:\n        base = ' '.join(toks[:-1])\n        if base in UNAMBIGUOUS_BASES:  # e.g. palmeiras, flamengo, ...\n            return base\n        # ambiguous base: keep suffix — key is s itself, normalized\n        return s\n    return s\n```\nWhere UNAMBIGUOUS_BASES = set of club bases that map to exactly one club in our curated canonical set. E.g. \"palmeiras\" ∈ set → \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"palmeiras\". \"atletico\" ∉ set → \"Atletico-MG\" → \"atletico mg\". \"america\" ∉ set → \"America-MG\" → \"america mg\", \"America RN\" → \"america rn\" ✓. \"botafogo\" ∉ → \"botafogo rj\"/\"botafogo pb\"/\"botafogo sp\" ✓. \"fluminense\" — bases {fluminense rj, fluminense pi}: \"Fluminense-PI\" is a tiny Piauí club that only ever appears WITH suffix; bare \"Fluminense\" = RJ giant. Decision: treat \"fluminense\" ∈ UNAMBIGUOUS_BASES → maps \"fluminense\" (RJ); \"fluminense pi\" keeps suffix → distinct key ✓; \"Fluminense De Feira\" → slug \"fluminense de feira\" — no UF suffix → own key ✓ (add explicit alias \"fluminense de feira\" stays). Wait — \"Fluminense De Feira\": last token \"feira\" not UF → returns \"fluminense de feira\" ✓ distinct. \n\nBut hold on: is there a risk \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → toks[-1]=\"rj\" is UF, base \"vasco da gama\" ∈ ALIASES → \"vasco\" ✓. \"Nacional AM\" → \"nacional am\" (ambiguous base kept) ✓. \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional uru\" ✓. \"Santos AP\" → \"santos ap\" ✓ distinct from \"santos\". \"Santos\" bare → \"santos\" ✓. \"Santos Laguna\" (FIFA) → last token \"laguna\" not UF → \"santos laguna\" ✓ distinct.\n\nUNAMBIGUOUS_BASES (curated): palmeiras, flamengo, corinthians, santos, sao paulo, vasco (\"vasco da gama\"→alias), sport, bahia, ceara, fortaleza, vitoria, cruzeiro, coritiba, parana, chapecoense, avai, figueirense, criciuma, goias, gremio, internacional, fluminense, guarani?? — \"Guarani SP\" (SP) and \"Guarani de Juazeiro\" (base \"guarani de juazeiro\" distinct) — but ALSO novo file has \"Guarani\" bare (2003 era, Campinas-SP) → base \"guarani\" unambiguous? There's only one \"guarani\" club with that exact base (Guarani-SP). Add. \"portuguesa\" → Portuguesa-SP (\"Portuguesa RJ\" is a tiny club, always suffixed) add. \"juventude\", \"caxias\" (\"Caxias RS\" → \"caxias\"; base unambiguous since \"caxias\" only RS club — but there's \"Duque De Caxias RJ\" base \"duque de caxias\" distinct ✓). \"nautico\" (Náutico-PE; \"Nautico RR\" suffixed ✓). \"remo\", \"paysandu\", \"sampaio correa\", \"ceara\", \"csa\", \"crb\", \"abc\", \"america rn\"?? base \"america\" ambiguous ✓ already. \"athletic club mg\"?? — \"Athletic Club MG\" (MG club, São João del Rei) — base \"athletic club\" — hmm appears only as \"Athletic Club MG\" → key \"athletic club mg\" fine.\nAlso \"atletico acreano\"/\"atletico alagoinhas\"/\"atletico goianiense\" — explicit aliases: \"atletico goianiense\" → \"atletico go\". \"atletico acreano\" → own key \"atletico acreano\" (distinct, fine). \n\nOK — and \"Athletico\" alone appears in Libertadores (\"Athletico\" 2019+ shorthand?) — I saw \"Athletico\" in Libertadores team list! slug \"athletico\" — no UF suffix → key \"athletico\" ≠ \"atletico pr\". Need alias: \"athletico\" → \"atletico pr\". Add to ALIASES.\n\nLet me also confirm Libertadores Brazilian club variants: \"Atlético-MG\", \"Flamengo\", \"Palmeiras\", \"Santos\", \"Grêmio\", \"Atlético-PR\"? etc. And \"Barcelona-EQU\" → \"barcelona equ\" (EQU not UF) ✓. \"Olimpia-PAR\" → \"par\" not UF → \"olimpia par\" ✓. \"Nacional (PAR)\" → \"nacional par\" ✓ — consistent.\n\nWhat about Copa do Brasil odd name \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\"? slug: parenthetical → token: \"boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira rj\" — ugh, the paren content becomes tokens; last token \"rj\" is UF; base = \"boavista sport club antigo esporte clube barreira\" — not in aliases → returns whole thing. Need better: DROP parenthetical content entirely for domestic names, but KEEP for foreign disambiguation? Conflict. Alternative: DROP paren content when it doesn't end with a recognized country code? The foreign ones are \"(URU)\", \"(PAR)\", \"(EQU)\" — 3-letter country codes; the domestic one is long text \"antigo Esporte Clube Barreira\". Rule: if paren content (slug) is a single token of ≤4 letters → keep as token; else drop. \"antigo esporte clube barreira\" → multi-token → drop → \"boavista sport club rj\" → base \"boavista sport club\" ∈ ALIASES → \"boavista\" ✓. \n\nAlso \"River (PI)\" → paren \"pi\" single ≤4 token → keep → \"river pi\" ✓ distinct from \"River AC\" → \"river ac\" ✓. Hmm wait \"River (PI)\" slug: \"river pi\". And there's bare \"River Plate\" in Libertadores → \"river plate\" distinct ✓.\n\nNow display names: build DISPLAY override map for major clubs; fallback = title-cased most-common raw. I'll build programmatically at load: for each key, Counter of raw names; pick most common; prefer variant with accents if within top counts. Plus curated overrides for the big ~40 clubs to get pretty names (\"São Paulo\", \"Atlético-MG\", \"Athletico-PR\"? — display for atletico pr: \"Athletico-PR\" (modern) or \"Atlético-PR\"? I'll use \"Athletico-PR\"... hmm, both used. Actually most common raw in data: Brasileirao_Matches uses \"Atletico-PR\" (2012-2018 file) — novo uses \"Athletico-PR\" (2019+). Counts similar. I'll curate \"Athletico-PR\". Eh — either fine. Curate it.)\n\nDATE parsing: handle \"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS\", \"YYYY-MM-DD\", \"DD/MM/YYYY\". BRFB date \"2023-09-24\" ISO ✓. Store date objects (or ISO strings — date objects better for range queries; store both? just date).\n\nFIFA parsing: Age/Overall/Potential/Jersey Number → int (some may be empty → None); Height \"5'7\" (feet'inches) and Weight \"159lbs\" — keep raw strings, parse to cm/kg? Nice-to-have; I'll parse to cm/kg floats where possible. Actually keep it simple: store raw + parsed helper. Skip parsing heights to cm — not needed by spec examples. Store as-is.\n\nValue/Wage \"€110.5M\"/\"€565K\" — skip (not required).\n\nNow — the Dataset class:\n\n```python\nclass Dataset:\n    matches: list[Match]\n    players: list[Player]\n    team_display: dict[key, str]\n    matches_by_team: defaultdict[key, list[Match]]\n    players_by_club: defaultdict[key, list[Player]]\n    brazilian_clubs: set[key]   # teams appearing in domestic competitions\n    competitions: dict[comp_name, CompetitionInfo]  # seasons, counts, sources\n```\n\nMatch fields: date (date|None), home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals (int|None), season (int|None), competition (str), stage (str|None), round (str|None), source (str), plus extras dict for BRFB stats (corners/shots/attacks/halftime).\n\nHmm — for BRFB, ht_result/at_result are half-time GOALS (floats like 0.0) despite the name suggesting result. \"ht_result, at_result | Half-time results\" with values DRAW/DRAW in row 1?? Wait the first BRFB row: `ht_diff,at_diff,ht_result,at_result` = `0.0,0.0,DRAW,DRAW`. So ht_result = \"DRAW\" string! And ht_diff = 0.0. OK so ht_result is W/D/L-ish string. I'll store extras: home_corner, away_corner, home_shots, away_shots, home_attack, away_attack, total_corners, ht_diff, ht_result, at_result, time. Only for BRFB matches. Keep in `extras` dict.\n\nDedup for display: as discussed, dedup key (competition, season, home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals) with source priority. But WAIT — two-legged cup ties where BOTH legs somehow have same home team AND same score... e.g. 2017 Copa r7: \"Botafogo 0-0 Flamengo\" and \"Flamengo 1-0 Botafogo\" — different homes ✓. 2013 r7: \"Goiás 1-2 Flamengo\" / \"Flamengo 2-1 Goiás\" ✓ different homes. Fine.\n\nBUT — league: 2015 Serie A in BOTH novo and Brasileirao files: Flamengo hosts Fluminense once; key (comp, 2015, flamengo, fluminense, hg, ag) — if scores agree between files → dedup to 1 ✓. If scores disagree → 2 rows ✗ (data error case, acceptable). Also BRFB 2015 Serie A — third copy, dedups ✓. What about matches BRFB has that others don't (Serie B etc.)? Only BRFB source → no dedup issue ✓.\n\nHmm — actually wait: will scores agree between novo and Brasileirao_Matches for 2012-2019? Both purport to be the same real matches, so yes unless one dataset has errors. Spot-check a few in tests. If a systematic disagreement exists, standings from canonical source remain correct (they use ONE source). Display dedup might show dupes occasionally — acceptable. Let me spot-check overlap scores now.\n\nAlso — \"source\" priority for dedup: Brazilian_Cup_Matches > BR-Football for Copa do Brasil? The Brazilian_Cup file is the purpose-built one → priority: [Libertadores_Matches, Brasileirao_Matches, Brazilian_Cup_Matches, novo_campeonato_brasileiro, BR-Football-Dataset]. \n\nCanonical source for standings per (competition, season): pick source with most matches; tie → priority order. Let me verify 2023 Serie A top team & 2020 bottom 4 & score agreement now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d69b897001ySsiVJYF9WJpKA","sessionID":"ses_fa29f8b64ffekf1eHikDs909KG","messageID":"msg_05d67cd24001hxzRF4WhXcSZdK"}}
