ALIASES[s]\n    toks = s.split()\n    if toks and toks[-1].upper() in UF:\n        base = ' '.join(toks[:-1])\n        if base in BASES: return base\n    return s\n\ndef load(path, hcol, acol, hg, ag, dcol, dparse, scol=None, tcol=None, tval=None):\n    rows = []\n    with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n            if tcol and row[tcol] != tval: continue\n            try:\n                hgi, agi = int(float(row[hg])), int(float(row[ag]))\n            except (ValueError, TypeError):\n                hgi = agi = None\n            d = dparse(row[dcol]) if row[dcol] else None\n            s = int(row[scol]) if scol and row[scol] not in ('', 'NA') else (int(d[:4]) if d else None)\n            if s is None: continue\n            rows.append((d, key(row[hcol]), key(row[acol]), hgi, agi, s))\n    return rows\n\ndef iso(d): return d[:10]\ndef brd(d):\n    dd, mm, yy = d.split('/'); return f\"{yy}-{mm}-{dd}\"\n\na = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv','home_team','away_team','home_goal','away_goal','datetime', iso, scol='season')\nfA = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv','home','away','home_goal','away_goal','date', iso, tcol='tournament', tval='Serie A')\n\ndef table(rows, year):\n    pts = {}\n    n = 0\n    for (d,h,aw,hg,ag,s) in rows:\n        if s != year or hg is None: continue\n        n += 1\n        for t in (h,aw): pts.setdefault(t, [0,0,0,0,0,0])\n        if hg>ag: pts[h][0]+=3; pts[h][1]+=1; pts[aw][3]+=1\n        elif hg<ag: pts[aw][0]+=3; pts[aw][1]+=1; pts[h][3]+=1\n        else: pts[h][0]+=1; pts[aw][0]+=1; pts[h][2]+=1; pts[aw][2]+=1\n        pts[h][4]+=hg; pts[h][5]+=ag; pts[aw][4]+=ag; pts[aw][5]+=hg\n    return n, sorted(pts.items(), key=lambda kv:(-kv[1][0], -(kv[1][4]-kv[1][5]), -kv[1][4]))\n\nn, t2020 = table(a, 2020)\nprint(\"2020 brfile scored matches:\", n)\nfor t,v in t2020[-4:]: print(\"  2020 bottom:\", t, v)\nfor t,v in t2020[:2]: print(\"  2020 top:\", t, v)\n\nn2, t2022 = table(a, 2022)\nprint(\"2022 brfile scored:\", n2, \"top:\", t2022[0])\nn3, t2022f = table(fA, 2022)\nprint(\"2022 brfb scored:\", n3, \"top:\", t2022f[0])\nn4, t2021f = table(fA, 2021)\nprint(\"2021 brfb scored:\", n4, \"top:\", t2021f[0])\nn5, t2021 = table(a, 2021)\nprint(\"2021 brfile scored:\", n5, \"top:\", t2021[0])\n# vasco key in 2020 brfile?\nkeys = {r[1] for r in a if r[5]==2020} | {r[2] for r in a if r[5]==2020}\nprint(\"vasco in 2020 keys:\", 'vasco' in keys)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788273766589,"end":1788273766592}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting — 82 rows with NA goals (81 in 2022, 1 in 2016). These are real fixtures with unknown scores (some 2022 matches recorded without scores in this dataset). My design: keep these matches in the dataset (searchable — \"Palmeiras played X on date\" works) but mark goals as None and EXCLUDE from statistics/standings. Good — that's why Bahia 2020 showed 27... no wait, the NA rows are 2022 (81) and 2016 (1) — none in 2020. So Bahia 2020 27-match mystery remains!\n\nHmm wait, let me recompute. Actually let me recount Bahia 2020 in brfile by home+away. The Counter above says ALL teams have 38 in 2020. But 'bahia' [28, 8, 4, 15] sums to 27. So 11 Bahia matches vanished. OHHH — I bet the issue is my `load()` skipping rows where `int(d[:4])`... no. OR — the 2020 bottom-4 print came from `table(a, 2020)` where `a` rows are (d, h, aw, hg, ag, s) — s = int(d[:4]) — for brfile d = row['datetime'][:10]... wait no, I passed dparse=iso which returns d[:10] = \"2020-...\" → s=int(2020). Hmm fine.\n\nWAIT. Actually — I passed season from date, but the CSV has a 'season' column. Are there 2020-season matches with 2021 dates (COVID delays!)? YES! The 2020 Brasileirão ended in FEBRUARY 2021 (COVID). So matches from the 2020 season dated 2021-01/02 get season=2021 in my date-derived season! That splits the 2020 season: 2020-date matches (most) + 2021-date matches (final rounds, ~11 per team) counted as \"2021\". That's why Bahia has 27 and why 2021 season would show extra matches (380+11×10... messy).\n\nFIX: use the file's own season column when available (brfile, cup, libertadores have 'season'; novo has 'Ano'; BRFB has none — derive from date). For BRFB, date-derived season could similarly split COVID-delayed 2020 season — BRFB 2020 Serie A shows 264 matches (incomplete anyway). Use date-derived for BRFB (best available) — with the caveat. Copa do Brasil 2020: rounds dated up to 2021-03 (finals) — the file's season column says 2020 ✓ use column.\n\nSo the Match model gets season from source column where present.\n\nAlso note the 2016 Chapecoense NA row: the tragic Chapecoense plane crash — the final match(es) of 2016 were not played (awarded). Keep as fixture without score.\n\nNow — team name variants in brfile 2022: \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" → slug \"red bull bragantino sp\" → base \"red bull bragantino\" not in BASES → key \"red bull bragantino sp\"... vs elsewhere \"Bragantino\" / \"RB Bragantino\" / \"Bragantino-SP\". Need aliases: \"red bull bragantino sp\"→'bragantino', \"bragantino sp\"→'bragantino', \"rb bragantino\"→'bragantino', \"red bull brasil\"→ own key (different club actually — Red Bull Brasil was the predecessor; treat as separate, fine).\n\nLet me also check 'operario' handling: BRFB has \"Operario PR\" and \"Operario MS\"/\"Operario FC MS\" and \"Operario MT\" — my alias 'operario'→'operario pr' would be WRONG if bare \"Operario\" refers to a different club... In BRFB, is there a bare \"Operario\"? The list showed 'Operario FC MS', 'Operario MS', 'Operario MT', 'Operario PR' — all suffixed ✓. novo/other files? Let me check for bare \"Operario\" occurrences — actually since all occurrences are suffixed, the alias never triggers. Remove risk: only alias bare 'operario' if it appears... skip that alias.\n\nLet me now also double-check 2020 season with season-column fix → Bahia should have 38 matches, and bottom-4 should be Coritiba, Botafogo, Goiás, Vasco (matching reality). And 2022 season will have 81 scoreless matches (excluded from stats — fine; standings note \"calculated from N scored matches\").\n\nOne more check: BRFB date-derived season — 2020 Serie A has 264 matches (partial); any BRFB 2020-season matches dated 2021? Probably (season ended Feb 2021). They'd count as 2021 → BRFB 2021 Serie A shows 491?! (380 normal + ~111 from delayed 2020 rounds). That pollutes 2021 BRFB standings! But standings use BEST source per season: 2021 → brfile has 380 (full, scored) vs BRFB 491 → BRFB WINS by count → WRONG standings for 2021! \n\nFix: canonical-source selection shouldn't blindly pick most matches. Better: pick source whose team-count and per-team match counts look most like a complete round-robin: e.g., source where max team matches == expected (2×(teams-1))? Simpler heuristic: prefer the source with the most DISTINCT TEAMS matching a clean league shape, or just use FIXED priority per competition: Serie A: [Brasileirao_Matches, novo, BR-Football] — pick first with matches in that season, BUT brfile lacks 2023 (only BRFB has it). And 2003-2011 only novo. So priority order covers: 2003-2011 novo (brfile has none) ✓; 2012-2022 brfile ✓; 2023 BRFB ✓. The only cost: brfile 2022 has 81 scoreless matches (299 scored) vs BRFB 2022 (379) vs novo (none). Priority picks brfile 2022 with 299 scored matches — incomplete! Hmm. Expected: 380. 299/380 = 79% — \"calculated from 299 scored matches\". Meanwhile BRFB 2022 Serie A: 379 matches. Hmm, which is more accurate? BRFB had ~60 score disagreements vs brfile in overlapping years (BRFB less reliable). But 299 vs 379 coverage... For 2022, best available full data = BRFB (379). Champion 2022 = Palmeiras. From brfile 299 matches — Palmeiras likely still on top (computed earlier: palmeiras 66pts in 299 matches ✓ top). From BRFB 379 — let me check.\n\nOK here's a cleaner source-selection heuristic: score each (source, competition, season) by \"completeness\" = matches / expected where expected = 2×(n_teams-1) for leagues... complicated. Alternative pragmatic rule for Serie A leagues:\n- Filter to sources where per-team match counts are consistent (max team count ≤ 38 and ≥ 90% of teams have ≥ 0.9×max)... \n\nHonestly, simplest robust approach: pick the source with the most SCORED matches, but reject sources whose team-match profile is \"polluted\" — detect pollution: if a season has MORE matches than expected for the team count... BRFB 2021 Serie A: 491 matches with 20 teams → 24.5 matches/team... wait 491 matches → teams have varying counts; Flamengo would have 38 (2021) + 11 (delayed 2020) = 49. Detect: any team with > 38 matches (2×(20-1)) → polluted. So heuristic: for leagues, valid source-season if every team has ≤ 2×(n_teams−1) matches... for n_teams=20 → 38. BRFB 2021: some teams >38 → reject. BRFB 2020: 264 matches, all ≤38 ✓ valid but partial (teams ~26 matches) — for 2020, brfile (380, full) wins on count anyway ✓. 2022: brfile 299 scored ✓ all ≤38 ✓; BRFB 379 — need check pollution (BRFB 2022 could include delayed 2021 matches? No — 2021 season ended Dec 2021 normally. But BRFB 2020 delayed rounds → BRFB 2021 polluted as computed). For 2022 both valid; most scored wins → BRFB 379. Is BRFB 2022 champion = Palmeiras? Compute. If yes, good.\n\nLet me also verify: BRFB 2021 rejected by pollution → brfile 2021 (380) wins ✓.\n\nFor cups (Copa do Brasil, Libertadores): no round-robin expectation; pick priority source with most matches: Copa: Brazilian_Cup_Matches priority over BRFB (both 2014-2021); 2022-2023 BRFB only. Libertadores: Libertadores_Matches only (BRFB has no Libertadores — tournament values were only Copa do Brasil/Serie A/B/C ✓).\n\nFor Serie B/C: BRFB only.\n\nFor Serie A also: what about 2020 BRFB \"264 matches\" — fine, brfile wins (380).\n\nAlright — the pollution check: I'll implement league validation: compute per-team match counts; expected_cap = 2*(n_teams-1); if any team's count > expected_cap → mark polluted, exclude. Also prefer more scored matches. Tie → priority. Edge: 2003/2004 had 46 matches per team (24+2 teams... 2003: 552 matches, 24 teams? 46×24/2 = 552 ✓ so cap = 2*(24-1)=46 ✓ works generically).\n\nLet me verify 2022 BRFB champion + the pollution logic + 2020 season-fixed bottom 4, then I'm confident to write the real code. Also check the 'Vasco' 2020 key and Botafogo.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05d6c304b001YfG9lbamhnrQVw","sessionID":"ses_fa29f8b64ffekf1eHikDs909KG","messageID":"msg_05d6b40cf001nEKydH5uMW08rp"}}
